    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas flows through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it cycles back inside to soak up more heat. Because heat is constantly removed, the inside stays cold, keeping your food fresh. Insulation helps trap the cold air inside."
